export const CONTRACT_VERSION = "dc958fbd4520ac2fa37c0e78211ef7f363e8c13207a724e6172e1be672aeb01b";
export const CONTRACT_HTML = "<p><strong>Case ID:</strong> {{CASE_ID}}  \u00b7  <strong>Date:</strong> {{DATE}}</p>\n<p>This agreement is between:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Client:</strong> {{CLIENT}} (\"you\")</li>\n<li><strong>FoxLock Security</strong> (\"we\", \"FoxLock\")</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Please read it in full. You can ask questions, strike out anything you don't agree to, and withdraw at any time (section 11).</p>\n<h2>1. Why we are doing this</h2>\n<p>You have told us that someone has been sending emails with damaging allegations about you to people you know, including friends from different parts of your life. You want to understand:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>what information about you can be found publicly, and how;</li>\n<li>whether public information could show your connections to the people who were contacted;</li>\n<li>whether any of your accounts show signs of misuse;</li>\n<li>how to reduce what can be found about you, and how to protect yourself going forward.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We do <strong>not</strong> assume that any account was hacked or that information was obtained in any particular way. We report only what the evidence shows.</p>\n<h2>2. What you authorize</h2>\n<p>By signing, you authorize FoxLock to do the following <strong>for you and on your behalf</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Public footprint research</strong> on you: search engines, public social media profiles, your current and past usernames, public web pages, and Internet Archive copies of public pages.</li>\n<li><strong>Data-broker and people-search sites:</strong> search them for your listings and record what they show about you.</li>\n<li><strong>Breach-notification checks:</strong> submit the email addresses you list in section 4 to Have I Been Pwned to see whether they appear in known data breaches.</li>\n<li><strong>Your accounts, with you present:</strong> you sign in to your own accounts (including your old Hotmail/Microsoft account) on a FoxLock computer or your own device and type your password yourself. We then review security settings, sign-in history, recovery details, connected apps, forwarding and inbox rules with you, document what we find, and fix problems you approve.</li>\n<li><strong>Your devices, with you present:</strong> you operate your phone and laptop while we look for security problems together: account sessions, location sharing, unknown apps or profiles, and tracking or monitoring software.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence you provide:</strong> collect, copy and preserve messages, screenshots and files you give us, including the harassing emails and any security alerts.</li>\n<li><strong>Removal and opt-out requests:</strong> prepare and, <strong>after you approve each one</strong>, submit opt-out requests to data brokers, removal requests to search engines and website administrators, and abuse reports to platforms, in your name.</li>\n<li><strong>Documentation of the harassment:</strong> organize the harassing messages and the sender's accounts into an evidence package for platform abuse reports, police, and any lawyer you choose to work with (see section 3).</li>\n<li><strong>Working with your friends:</strong> contact the people you name in section 5, with your permission, to ask whether they will share the messages they received and review their own accounts. Each of them decides for themselves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If there is anything in this list you do not want, cross it out before signing.</p>\n<h2>3. What we will not do</h2>\n<p>These limits protect you and keep the evidence usable. They apply even if someone asks us to cross them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>We will not sign in to, or try to get into, any account that is not yours. That includes accounts belonging to your friends or to the person harassing you.</li>\n<li>We will not guess or test passwords, send phishing messages, take over sessions, or get around privacy settings or logins.</li>\n<li>We will not buy stolen data, or buy background reports about anyone other than you.</li>\n<li>We will not dig into your friends' lives. We look at their public information only as far as it shows a connection to you.</li>\n<li><strong>About the person harassing you:</strong> we document what he sent, the accounts and addresses he used, and anything he has posted publicly about you, so it can go to platforms, police or a lawyer. We will not look for his home address, workplace or family, track his location, contact him, or publish anything about him. If you need his identity or address for a protective order, that goes through a lawyer or the police, who have legal ways to get it.</li>\n<li>We will not change or delete anything in your accounts or devices without your OK for that change. Before any fix (removing a forwarding rule, signing out an unknown device, turning on two-factor sign-in), we screenshot what was there, so the evidence is kept.</li>\n<li>We will not submit any request to a third party in your name without your approval of that specific request.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>4. What information we use</h2>\n<p>The names, usernames, email addresses and cities we research are the ones you give us on our intake checklist. We only search or submit what you list there, and you can add or remove anything at any time with a text.</p>\n<h2>5. People connected to the case</h2>\n<p>We only contact a friend after you tell us to, and each person decides for themselves whether to take part. For your friends, we only look at <strong>public</strong> information that shows their connection to you, and we check a friend's email address only with their consent.</p>\n<h2>6. Passwords and codes</h2>\n<p>You type your own passwords. We will never ask for, write down or store your passwords, one-time codes, recovery codes or recovery keys. If you sign in on a FoxLock computer, we will use a separate browser profile, the browser will not save your password, and at the end of each session we will sign out with you and remove that computer from your account's list of devices.</p>\n<h2>7. Confidentiality and sensitive information</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Everything you tell us, and everything we find, is confidential. We share it only with you, or with others <strong>you</strong> choose (for example police or a lawyer).</li>\n<li>You may tell us things that are private or sensitive. Tell us anything you want kept out of the written report and we will leave it out. Where it matters to the findings, we will write about it in general terms only, after agreeing the wording with you.</li>\n<li>If we come across private personal content while working on your devices or accounts, we will not read beyond what the review needs, and we will not copy it.</li>\n<li>The written report can be produced in two versions: a full one for you, and a redacted one (names, emails and phone numbers removed) you can share.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>8. Storage, security and retention</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Case data is kept on FoxLock's computers, in encrypted storage that only the people working on your case can access.</li>\n<li>FoxLock uses third-party software and online services to organize and analyze case data and to draft the report. We never sell or publish your information, or share it with anyone for their own use.</li>\n<li>Every file you give us is preserved unchanged, with a fingerprint (a SHA-256 hash) so anyone can later check it was not altered. All research activity is logged.</li>\n<li>We keep case data for <strong>90 days</strong> after delivering the final report, unless you ask us to delete it sooner or to keep it longer (for example, for a court case). Then it is deleted, and we confirm this to you in writing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>9. Payments</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FoxLock charges you no fee for this work.</strong></li>\n<li>The only payments involved are out-of-pocket costs for things like data-broker removal services, or a paid report about <strong>you</strong> from a data broker so we can see exactly what it holds.</li>\n<li>We tell you what each cost is for and how much it is <strong>before</strong> anything is bought. Nothing is bought without your written OK (a text is fine), and you get the receipt.</li>\n<li>You can choose not to pay for anything. We will then use free methods only and tell you what that leaves undone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>10. What to expect</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>We can only find what is publicly available or what you show us. Not finding something does not prove it doesn't exist.</li>\n<li>A breach listing shows your address was in a leaked dataset. It does not show that anyone got into your account.</li>\n<li>Removing information stops new people from finding it. It does not erase copies someone has already saved.</li>\n<li>FoxLock is not law enforcement and not a law firm, and this agreement is not legal advice. If you are in danger, call 911. We can help you prepare evidence for police or a lawyer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>11. Duration and withdrawal</h2>\n<p>This agreement runs from the date signed until the final report is delivered, or {{END_DATE}}, whichever comes first. You can withdraw at any time, by text or in writing. We will then stop all work immediately and, if you ask, delete your case data.</p>";
